msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "{ph0}Learning the next experiment{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="#re-and-rl">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:254
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "{ph0}RE and reinforcement learning{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="#oracle">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:255
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "{ph0}When the oracle is wrong{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="#comparison">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:256
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "{ph0}A comparison worth running{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="#future-agent">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:257
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "{ph0}What kind of agent?{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:265
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "What better models changed"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:266
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Since August 2026, I have been exploring how much reverse engineering an agent can "
"carry out with access to a program and a way to check its work. Most of my experience "
"comes from reconstructing games, especially Touhou. As I have changed models, the "
"repositories have kept a record of what each session managed to recover."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:274
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Much of that work aims at exact reconstruction: we compile the maintained source under"
" a pinned historical toolchain and check whether it reproduces the target function. "
"When a later model succeeds where earlier work stalled, the source changes show what "
"made that progress possible."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:281
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "The temporary values had to stay alive together"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:282
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Much of a TH09 attack function's logic had already been reconstructed, but the "
"compiler still stored temporary results differently from the original. The remaining "
"problem was how long those results needed to stay alive and when their storage could "
"be reused."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/de63c0f9121963f3a6b1ab5471c4878625fd3425"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/52929ed7c299f1a5f29cc13964e22510145fd616"
#. >
#. {ph3}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:289
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"A {ph0}GPT-6-Sol checkpoint{ph1} recorded this mismatch after testing several ordinary"
" source arrangements. When {ph2}GPT-6.1-Sol returned to the problem{ph3}, it found a "
"useful hypothesis about how long those temporary values needed to stay alive."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:303
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"The function produced temporary results and copied them into animation positions. In "
"C++, a temporary result stays alive until the expression using it finishes. Grouping "
"the real operations into one expression kept those results alive together, so the "
"compiler could no longer reuse the same storage for all of them."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:310
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"One result still shared storage with a value used later for collision checks. Giving "
"that value its own lifetime resolved the remaining overlap. After several targeted "
"experiments, {ph0}a clean rebuild confirmed that the full function matched{ph1}."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:319
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"What interests me is how the discovery and the tests worked together. The lifetime "
"hypothesis gave the agent a new direction; compiler feedback helped it refine that "
"hypothesis into a source arrangement that matched."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:325
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "A different control-flow graph"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/f8d12d72aaf02212ee1b585a8f3f86d7ed000269"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:326
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"A second TH09 function shows why that new direction can matter. Here, the obstacle was"
" how several branches shared their final work. {ph0}GPT-6.1-Sol's earlier work{ph1} "
"got parts of the function to match, but the branches still joined up differently from "
"the original."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/da33a646221198ceb7f3df2b96e2ca0e9b2f705f"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/5f825b28a862f8e1a6d4aea2f78a42f04c09a1c0"
#. >
#. {ph3}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:337
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}GPT-6-Astra changed the surrounding control-flow graph{ph1}. The main paths now "
"led to a shared final update and return. In that source structure, the compiler "
"combined the branches in the same way as the original. The complete function matched, "
"and a {ph2}separate rebuild check confirmed it{ph3}."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:349
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Changing the representation opened a more productive search space.{ph1} Both "
"cases give me a reason to take general model capability seriously: an agent can keep "
"trying local variations for a long time before finding a better way to express the "
"surrounding work."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:358
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"At the same time, the later sessions started with earlier source and recorded "
"failures. That accumulated work was part of their advantage. The history lets us "
"inspect the discoveries and their checks; a controlled comparison would help separate "
"the effect of the model from the experience it inherited."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:365
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"This is where the question becomes interesting to me. Some of the progress came from "
"finding a new representation, while much of the remaining work involved testing source"
" forms against a familiar compiler. How much needs broad reasoning ability, and how "
"much could a model learn through repeated experience with that compiler and code?"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:375
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Why might a specialist do well?"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:376
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Touhou gives us a concrete reason to expect that experience to help. Much of the game "
"code comes from the same author, ZUN, so recurring habits are a reasonable starting "
"assumption. The repositories back that up with useful examples: related loops and "
"helper arrangements have supplied hypotheses across games."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:383
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Those source patterns become more specific when we include the compiler. Under a "
"particular version and set of options, small changes in source can produce repeatable "
"differences in machine code. Some of those differences are surprisingly obscure."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#. {ph2}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th095/commit/26bb64bdb093a13f73abd0ee8ae7ce199ee4658f"
#. >
#. {ph3}: <code>Supervisor::LoadDat</code>
#. {ph4}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:389
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"In TH095, for example, {ph0}even the name used for a local variable could change where"
" VC7.1 stored it{ph1}. Moving the declaration alone did not solve the layout, but an "
"earlier background function had revealed a naming pattern that affected the compiler's"
" choice. That gave us a more promising hypothesis for {ph2}the reconstruction of "
"{ph3}{ph4}."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:403
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Reusing that pattern for the file-size variable produced the required layout without "
"adding storage. The full function then matched."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:408
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"This is the sort of narrow detail a general model may not know when it arrives. A "
"specialist trained on this compiler could encounter many such cases and learn which "
"source variations are worth testing first."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:414
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"The opportunity is especially clear in final matching problems where we already "
"understand the behavior. We are then asking which ordinary source form this compiler "
"turns into the observed instructions. Specialization could help the model propose "
"those forms more efficiently, while the target still determines which hypotheses "
"survive."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04/commit/6dce0a79d5b931f0e2a08475b90e0c33d2321b98"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:422
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"The useful patterns would have to retain their context, though. TH08 uses an earlier "
"Visual C++ version than TH095 and TH09. TH04 uses a different DOS-era compiler, and "
"its {ph0}player reconstruction{ph1} needed different hypotheses about source patterns "
"and how the compiled code was combined into an executable. Shared authorship gives us "
"a prior, but each target tells us whether it applies. Distinguishing those contexts is"
" part of what a specialist would need to learn."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:438
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Where should that experience live?"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="../touhou-reconstruction/">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:439
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Before deciding to teach those patterns through model training, I would look at the "
"knowledge we already keep in the repository. In the {ph0}previous essay{ph1}, I wrote "
"about it becoming the project's working memory. That memory includes compiler lessons,"
" along with the source and failed experiments that made them useful."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:448
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"By retrieving that record, an agent can start with a known result and inspect how we "
"reached it. It can also avoid alternatives that have already failed in the same "
"context. The starting point improves without changing the model's weights."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/1eb843383941d00065622a9e353146c4bb96979e"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:454
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"When reconstructing TH09's enemy-spawn function, a {ph0}GPT-6-Luna-max session{ph1} "
"drew a hypothesis from TH08's corresponding source family. That earlier source "
"suggested how to structure the loop and handle failures within it. TH09's own evidence"
" supplied the game-specific details, and clean rebuilds confirmed the complete match."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:465
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}\"The model did not know that compiler quirk\" and \"the model could not use it\""
" are different problems.{ph1} Retrieval may solve the first, but turning that fact "
"into a useful candidate still takes work. And if the candidate fails, the model must "
"decide what the result means for its next attempt. Knowing the relevant pattern is "
"only the beginning of that process."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:475
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"For that reason, I would include a smaller general model with good retrieval in any "
"comparison. Giving it the same library of experience would help us identify what "
"training adds: better candidate generation, better decisions after feedback, or both."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:483
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Learning the next useful experiment"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:484
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Those decisions after feedback are what I would most like to study. In agentic "
"reconstruction, the model proposes an explanation and tests it against the program. "
"Even when the candidate fails, the result can narrow the remaining possibilities and "
"suggest a more useful next experiment."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:491
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Consider the temporary-results example again. Its final source shows the arrangement "
"that worked, but the route to that source contains more information. Each of the "
"following experiments helped identify what still needed to change."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:504
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Experiment"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:505
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "What its result taught us"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:510
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Try a different way to copy the results"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:511
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "The copy code changes, but the function still differs."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:516
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Use the returned values directly"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:517
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Copying follows the original pattern, but temporary storage still differs."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:523
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Keep the temporary results alive together"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:524
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"They get separate storage, but one still overlaps a value used later for collision "
"checks."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:530
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Keep the collision-check value separate"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:531
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "A clean rebuild confirms the complete match."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:536
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Selected experiments from the attack-function reconstruction above, grouped by what "
"they taught us."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:540
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"A training record that keeps this sequence can teach more than the final source alone."
" It connects each proposed change to an observed result and the next decision. Keeping"
" ineffective variations matters too: they tell a later agent which hypotheses have "
"already been tested in that context."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:547
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}I would want the model to learn how to use feedback.{ph1} A neutral result is a "
"reason to reconsider the variation; a changed mismatch may point to another cause. "
"When local edits stop being informative, the agent should be able to revisit the "
"source representation or inspect the evidence again."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:556
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Learning to make those choices requires autonomy. Sometimes the useful next step is "
"inspecting another caller rather than compiling another edit. A rigid sequence of "
"required rewrites would remove that choice, along with part of the behavior we want "
"the model to learn."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:565
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "Why RE is interesting for reinforcement learning"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:566
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Bounded reconstruction gives us a practical environment for studying these choices. "
"Once the target and compiler are pinned, many source experiments produce quick, "
"repeatable feedback. The agent can compile a candidate, inspect the mismatch and use "
"that observation to choose its next step."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:573
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Reinforcement learning could train that decision-making process through the feedback "
"it receives. A candidate accepted by a defined check gives us a concrete outcome, "
"while the sequence of attempts lets us observe how much work it took to reach it."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.203/" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2606.16162v1">
#. {ph3}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:579
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"There is already research supporting this direction. {ph0}LLM4Decompile{ph1} studies "
"models specialized for decompilation. {ph2}AutoDecompiler{ph3} trains multi-turn "
"refinement using compiler and execution feedback. Their studies focus on functional "
"recovery. For this work, I would use them as a starting point for the related task of "
"finding source that matches a historical compiler's output."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/commit/af9cb1d3e839b15a78b47ac1701506083cc82847"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:591
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"To reach such a well-defined task, though, we may first need to establish what a "
"function owns or devise a new runtime measurement. In TH08, for example, a "
"{ph0}collision difference led back to how the original program handled floating-point "
"calculations{ph1}. Before the agent could test a useful correction, it had to work out"
" what to measure."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:602
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"This suggests another role for a broadly capable model: helping make the problem "
"checkable in the first place. Once that check exists, we can investigate how far a "
"learned search policy can get with it."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:610
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "When the oracle is wrong"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:611
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Once we train against that check, its coverage becomes part of what the model learns. "
"I call the reference or checker an oracle. Even if it gives a repeatable answer, we "
"still need to understand what that answer covers."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:617
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"TH08 showed how much can hide outside that coverage. Some reconstructed functions "
"passed their exact comparisons with wrong floating constants. The comparator adjusted "
"relocated addresses before checking instructions, but it checked the values behind "
"those addresses only when they had been explicitly recorded for verification. Other "
"literal references could pass without their values being examined."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/commit/a393f400786e4cc6cbb1000af0dad3276da12c78"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <code>1.0f</code>
#. {ph3}: <code>32.0f</code>
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:626
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"To close that gap, a {ph0}wider audit{ph1} found incorrect constants in functions that"
" had already been accepted. In one calculation, the reconstructed source added {ph2} "
"where the original added {ph3}."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:636
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}A learner rewarded by the old check could learn to produce the wrong "
"reconstruction.{ph1} We had not trained an RL model against this oracle; the observed "
"failure was in the check itself. But using that check as a reward would have made the "
"same blind spot part of the learning environment."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.05370v1" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:646
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"A functional test can have a similar blind spot. {ph0}Differential-fuzzing research on"
" LLM decompilers{ph1} has found behavior differences beyond the supplied tests. "
"Passing those tests therefore needs to be read in terms of the cases they exercise, "
"just as an instruction match needs to be read in terms of the bytes and data it "
"checks."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:656
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Before using either kind of check as a reward, I would test it with deliberate "
"failures. I would also keep final acceptance outside the candidate's editable source, "
"so the model has to satisfy the check through its reconstruction. Otherwise, something"
" like a byte array copied from the target could satisfy a naive score without "
"recovering useful source."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:664
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Improving the learner and improving its oracle belong to the same research "
"problem.{ph1} As the learner gets better at optimizing the feedback we give it, "
"understanding that feedback becomes more important too."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:674
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "A comparison worth running"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:675
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"With those checks in place, I would start the comparison on a fixed VC7.1 task family."
" First, I would establish baselines for a strong general model and a smaller one. "
"Adding repository knowledge to the smaller model would show what retrieval changes. "
"From there, supervised specialization and an RL-trained version would let us examine "
"what training adds."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:683
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"To make those comparisons informative, each model would receive the same target "
"evidence and tools. For the training-specific comparisons, I would keep the base model"
" and available knowledge fixed as well. Repeated trials would help distinguish a "
"reliable search strategy from a lucky candidate."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:690
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"I would then separate two starting conditions: reconstructing a function from its "
"instructions, and closing the last few differences in maintained source. A specialist "
"might be excellent at the second task while a general model does much of the work "
"needed to reach it. Measuring them separately would reveal that division of work."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:698
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Both tasks need genuinely held-out examples. Because related Touhou games share engine"
" code, holding out a game's name would still allow nearly identical functions to "
"appear in training. I would instead hold out source families and keep their accepted "
"answers out of both training and retrieval."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:705
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"On those examples, the outcome would be complete, independently validated matches. "
"Giving each model the same candidate or compile budget would help compare search "
"quality. Measuring elapsed time and total cost, including compilation, would tell us "
"whether that quality produces an efficient workflow. Training costs would need their "
"own accounting too."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:713
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Both views matter because a cheaper proposal can become an expensive workflow if it "
"causes more uninformative compiles. Conversely, a slower model may pay for itself by "
"finding a useful experiment sooner. The comparison should capture those costs."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/ab72fd77728293fc0beacfc656ca701b0e266016"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:719
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"Counting accepted results also keeps intermediate progress in perspective. One TH09 "
"{ph0}replay-save candidate{ph1} looked promising because the compiled function had the"
" expected size, but a full comparison still found substantial differences. Changes in "
"how the compiler arranges instructions can also make a candidate look closer without "
"resolving the underlying problem. Such diagnostics can guide the next experiment, but "
"I would validate any reward for \"getting closer\" against the full acceptance check."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:734
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"I would be interested in the pattern across these results. If a specialist does well "
"within a familiar compiler family, does that advantage survive a toolchain change? And"
" once both models have good retrieval, how much does training still add? The answers "
"would help us decide where each kind of model is most useful."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:743
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid "What kind of agent does this suggest?"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:744
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"If the models turn out to help at different stages, a useful arrangement might be a "
"broad agent with access to a specialist. Once a task becomes a familiar compiler "
"search, it could ask the specialist for candidates. If the results stop making sense, "
"the broader agent could return to the target evidence or investigate the measurement "
"itself."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:752
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"In that arrangement, specialization becomes a tool the agent can choose as the "
"investigation develops. The agent keeps the freedom to change direction that made the "
"earlier breakthroughs possible."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:757
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"This follows naturally from the industrial shift I described in the previous essay. "
"Being able to run many more experiments changes how we organize the work. It also "
"gives us a reason to ask which decisions need broad judgment and which improve through"
" repeated experience with a particular craft."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:764
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"For now, the strongest general model has earned its place in my workflow by opening "
"problems I had left unresolved. Those successes are what make me want to examine the "
"work more closely: how much depended on the model's breadth, and how much could be "
"handled by a model trained in this particular kind of reconstruction?"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html:772
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/reverse-engineering-models/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}The repositories give us a way to test the question.{ph1} I would like to use "
"that record to build the comparison and see what we learn."
msgstr ""

